  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nToday will be another fair weather day with increasing clouds\nand highs in the 60s after valley fog lifts. A wave of low \npressure passing to our south late today and tonight should \nremain to our south as per strong model consensus.\n\nTemperatures tonight should be warm enough to preclude frost in\nthe zones that are still active. \n\nOn Monday, a strong cold front sweeps across the CWA. The\npreceding air mass will be quite dry so we don't expect much in\nthe way of precipitation with the front. However, there could \nbe a line of gusty showers with the front in some areas, \nespecially northern zones. Winds in the wake of the front during\nMonday afternoon should gust 25 to 35 MPH, with some forecast \nsounding profiles supporting a few 40 MPH gusts. The gusts will \ngradually wane overnight, but should stay up enough to preclude \nfrost development in the zones that are still active.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nTuesday will be a cool, breezy, and crisp-feeling fall day as cold \nadvection advection continues following Monday's cold front. \nForecast soundings support gusts of 25 to 30 mph for much of the \nday, and wind chills could be in the 20s and 30s during morning \nwork/school commute time. So make sure to grab the coat. High \ntemperatures will be limited to the 50s south of the mountains and \n40s across the north. For hikers, higher elevations will likely see \nsubfreezing wind chills through the day.\n\nWinds ease Tuesday evening and Tuesday night as high pressure builds \noverhead. Skies will be mostly clear, and if winds can go light \nenough, good radiational cooling could bring the coldest night \nof the season so far with frost and possibly the first freeze\nfor many locations south of the mountains. Widespread lows in \nthe 30s are possible with some 20s in the northern valleys and \nmore sheltered locations. \n\nThe upper-air pattern indicates troughing over much of eastern\nCanada and extending somewhat into the Great Lakes and\nNortheast from mid to late week. Shortwaves could push a couple\nof fronts through the area to potentially bring some rain \nshowers, but virtually all ensemble members indicate only light\nrainfall amounts possible. Ahead of these fronts, winds will be\nmainly out of the south to southwest, so temperatures will start\nto recover. Highs Wednesday should be in the 50s to lower 60s\nand then guidance favors most in the 60s Thursday and Friday,\npossibly around 70 in southern NH. Ensembles then favor mostly\ndry conditions heading into next weekend with high pressure\nbuilding toward the Northeastern US.",
